{"format":"tvf-scene","v":1,"name":"example · near and far","loop":40,"ease":"","hold":0,"gravity":1,"wind":0,"trail":null,"camera":[{"t":0,"box":[-0.4392,-0.17952,0.2742,0.1842]}],"stops":[0.18,0.38,0.58,0.78],"beats":[{"title":"Fence posts on a plain","text":"Sixty-four fence posts a step apart, running straight away from you, as the number line holds them: every gap the same.\n\nStanding among them you do not see it that way. The first few are clear steps, one, two, three. Far out they crowd: you can tell the sixty-fourth is about twice as far as the thirty-second, and not much more. **Near things are read by their differences, far things by their ratios.**"},{"title":"Inside the corner: proportion","text":"The reader holds one length, *H*, here eight posts. Everything within it is laid out at true scale against it: a post half of *H* away goes halfway out. This is the **near field**, laid in proportion, and *H* is a ruler there.\n\nThe gold mark is the corner, where the reader's length ends."},{"title":"Past the corner: every doubling the same room","text":"A ruler with equal marks never ends, so the far posts cannot be laid the same way. Since nothing fixes *H*, a stretch from *d* to twice *d* should get the same room wherever it falls, and only one rule does that: the logarithm, starting at the corner with the same slope, 1 + ln(*d*/*H*).\n\nFrom 8 to 16, from 16 to 32, from 32 to 64: **each doubling gets the same room.** [The essay, §3](https://github.com/reportbase/wander/blob/main/papers/essays/04-near-and-far.md)"},{"title":"Reality in 2H","text":"The same addresses can be laid by length instead: a far post at 2 − *H*/*d*. Then the first doubling past the corner takes half of the next *H*, the second a quarter, the third an eighth, and the whole far field, out to infinity, fits between *H* and **2*H***.\n\nThe reader holds the same places either way, the same number each doubling. Laid by count they run on; laid by length they crowd toward a wall."},{"title":"H is a zoom","text":"Now let *H* be sixteen posts. Every post slides toward home along the same lay; nothing about the lay changes, only where the world falls on it. Sixteen posts are now in proportion, each given less room than before.\n\n**A larger H holds more at true scale and gives each thing less detail.** No single *H* holds every scale at once, so the best one puts the thing being read at the corner, as focusing an eye does. [H is a zoom, §11](https://github.com/reportbase/wander/blob/main/papers/essays/04-near-and-far.md)"}],"path":null,"items":[{"fill":{"color":"#f4f1ea","opacity":1},"stroke":null,"width":0,"open":false,"exact":{"x":[-0.9,0.9,0.9,-0.9],"y":[0.6,0.6,-0.6,-0.6],"r":null,"smooth":null,"ctrl":null,"holes":[]}},{"morph":0},{"morph":1},{"morph":2},{"morph":3},{"morph":8},{"morph":9},{"morph":6},{"morph":7},{"morph":10},{"morph":12},{"morph":16},{"morph":4},{"morph":18},{"morph":19},{"morph":20},{"morph":21},{"morph":22},{"morph":23},{"morph":24},{"morph":25},{"morph":26},{"morph":27},{"morph":28},{"morph":29},{"morph":30},{"morph":5},{"morph":31},{"morph":32},{"morph":33},{"morph":34},{"morph":35},{"morph":36},{"morph":37},{"morph":38},{"morph":39},{"morph":40},{"morph":41},{"morph":42},{"morph":43},{"morph":11},{"morph":13},{"morph":78},{"morph":14},{"morph":79},{"morph":15},{"morph":80},{"morph":17},{"morph":81},{"morph":44},{"morph":45},{"morph":46},{"morph":47},{"morph":48},{"morph":49},{"morph":50},{"morph":51},{"morph":52},{"morph":53},{"morph":54},{"morph":55},{"morph":56},{"morph":57},{"morph":58},{"morph":59},{"morph":60},{"morph":61},{"morph":62},{"morph":63},{"morph":64},{"morph":65},{"morph":66},{"morph":67},{"morph":68},{"morph":69},{"morph":70},{"morph":71},{"morph":72},{"morph":73},{"morph":74},{"morph":75},{"morph":76},{"morph":77}],"morphs":[{"exact":true,"open":true,"holes":null,"keys":[{"x":[-0.39,0.225],"y":[0.135,0.135],"r":[0,0],"c":[null,null]}],"fill":null,"inPlace":true,"lag":0,"fx":null,"own":{"parent":-1,"parentAt"